description = "A struct and a variant of another type of the file may share a name: written as a type, the name means the struct, and written as a value, it means the variant."

// The variant `Remote` carries the struct `Remote`. A name written where a
// type is wanted is never a constructor, so the payload, the parameter and the
// result all mean the struct, however many constructors are known when each
// is read.
@ cases.a-variant-carries-the-struct-of-its-name
source = ```tauto
pub(open) struct Remote { id: u64 }
pub enum Parent { Current
  Remote(Remote) }

pub fn get(p: Parent): Option(Remote) {
  match p {
    Remote(r) => Some(r)
    Current => None
  }
}

pub fn wrap(r: Remote): Parent { Parent::Remote(r) }

fn id_of(p: Parent): u64 {
  match get(p) {
    Some(r) => r.id
    None => 0
  }
}

show(&id_of(wrap(Remote { id: 7 })))
show(&id_of(Current))
```
types = "ok"
stdout = ```
7
0
```

// The order the two are declared in never decides: a variant declared before
// the struct of its name is the value `P(..)` means where its type is wanted,
// and `P { .. }` means the struct.
@ cases.a-variant-before-the-struct-of-its-name
source = ```nib
enum Other { P(Text) }
struct P { n: i64 }
let p: P := P { n: 42 }
let other: Other := P("other")
```
types = "ok"
lin = "ok"
